  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 50-year-old male with spinal cord stimulation (SCS) for chronic LBP is referred for physiotherapy. Which physiotherapy modality is ABSOLUTELY contraindicated in this patient?", "option_a": "Therapeutic exercise", "option_b": "Therapeutic ultrasound or diathermy over the implanted SCS device — electromagnetic interference may damage the device or cause tissue heating", "option_c": "Cold pack therapy", "option_d": "Manual therapy to uninvolved regions", "correct_option_index": 1, "rationale": "Therapeutic ultrasound and diathermy (shortwave, microwave) are absolutely contraindicated over or near implanted electronic devices (SCS, pacemakers, insulin pumps) due to the risk of electromagnetic interference causing device malfunction or excessive tissue heating around metal components."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist is treating a 42-year-old female with bilateral hand OA. She has difficulty opening jars and managing buttons. Which approach MOST effectively addresses her hand function limitations?", "option_a": "Passive wrist mobilization only", "option_b": "Joint protection education (energy conservation, pacing, joint-sparing techniques), adaptive equipment provision (jar openers, button hooks), and progressive hand strengthening within pain-free tolerance", "option_c": "Complete hand rest for 6 months", "option_d": "Aggressive strengthening against maximal resistance", "correct_option_index": 2, "rationale": "Hand OA management combines joint protection education (preserving joint integrity through activity modification), adaptive equipment (reducing joint stress during daily tasks), and progressive strengthening (within pain-free tolerance to maintain function). This multimodal approach is the evidence-based standard."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist assesses a 29-year-old elite female volleyball player for injury prevention. She demonstrates a knee valgus angle of >10 degrees during the drop vertical jump task. The physiotherapist identifies this as a modifiable risk factor. Which intervention MOST effectively reduces the ACL injury risk associated with dynamic knee valgus in female athletes?", "option_a": "Knee bracing during all sport activities", "option_b": "Neuromuscular training programs targeting hip external rotation, abduction strength, and jump-landing mechanics (e.g., FIFA 11+, PEP program)", "option_c": "Bilateral HVLAT knee mobilization", "option_d": "Quadriceps stretching only", "correct_option_index": 3, "rationale": "Neuromuscular training programs (FIFA 11+, PEP, KIPP) that include plyometric, strengthening, and balance exercises addressing dynamic knee valgus and landing mechanics are the most effective ACL injury prevention strategies in female athletes, reducing ACL injury rates by 50% in RCTs."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 55-year-old male with knee OA and a BMI of 31 is counseled about the role of weight loss in managing his condition. He asks how much weight loss is needed to make a meaningful difference. Which statement is MOST evidence-based?", "option_a": "Only bariatric surgery producing >30% weight loss is meaningful", "option_b": "A weight loss of 5-10% of body weight (approximately 4-8 kg in his case) produces clinically meaningful reductions in knee OA pain and functional improvement", "option_c": "Weight loss has no impact on knee OA symptoms", "option_d": "Any weight below BMI 25 is required for symptom improvement", "correct_option_index": 0, "rationale": "Studies (Messier IDEA trial) demonstrate that weight loss of as little as 5-10% of body weight produces clinically meaningful pain reduction and functional improvement in knee OA, through both biomechanical (reduced joint loading) and anti-inflammatory (reduced adipokines) mechanisms."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist is treating a 38-year-old female with lateral ankle instability following recurrent ankle sprains. She uses the Cumberland Ankle Instability Tool (CAIT) to assess severity. She scores 18/30. Which interpretation is MOST accurate?", "option_a": "18/30 is a normal score indicating no instability", "option_b": "A CAIT score of 18/30 indicates chronic ankle instability (cutoff score ≤25 suggests chronic instability); lower scores indicate greater instability severity", "option_c": "The CAIT is not validated for ankle instability assessment", "option_d": "CAIT scores below 10 only indicate instability", "correct_option_index": 1, "rationale": "The CAIT is a validated 9-item questionnaire (max 30) for chronic ankle instability (CAI). A score of 25 or below indicates the presence of CAI. A score of 18 suggests moderate severity of chronic ankle instability, guiding rehabilitation focus on proprioception, balance, and peroneal strengthening."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 33-year-old male with a knee injury is assessed. The pivot shift test is performed: the examiner applies valgus stress and internal rotation to the extended knee, then flexes the knee. A palpable clunk is felt at approximately 30 degrees of flexion as the iliotibial band moves posterior to the lateral femoral condyle axis. What does a positive pivot shift test indicate?", "option_a": "Lateral meniscus tear", "option_b": "Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) deficiency causing anterolateral rotatory instability", "option_c": "PCL tear", "option_d": "MCL laxity", "correct_option_index": 2, "rationale": "The pivot shift test assesses ACL integrity by detecting anterolateral rotatory instability. The palpable 'clunk' occurs when the anterolaterally subluxed tibia reduces at approximately 30 degrees of flexion as the IT band changes from an extensor to a flexor. A positive pivot shift is highly specific for ACL deficiency."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist performs a Lachman test on a 25-year-old male soccer player following a non-contact knee injury. There is 6 mm of anterior tibial translation with a soft endpoint. The patient reports immediate severe pain and hearing a 'pop' at the time of injury. Which ligament is MOST likely involved?", "option_a": "Posterior cruciate ligament", "option_b": "Anterior cruciate ligament", "option_c": "Medial collateral ligament", "option_d": "Posterolateral corner", "correct_option_index": 3, "rationale": "The Lachman test is the most sensitive clinical test for ACL integrity. >3mm anterior tibial translation with a soft endpoint indicates ACL rupture. The clinical triad of non-contact deceleration mechanism, audible 'pop,' and immediate hemarthrosis is classic for ACL tear. Sensitivity of Lachman is 86% and specificity 91%."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 45-year-old female receives physiotherapy for chronic shoulder pain. She is currently on selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) for depression. A platelet-rich plasma (PRP) injection is discussed. Which concern regarding SSRIs and platelet function is MOST relevant?", "option_a": "SSRIs enhance platelet aggregation, making PRP more effective", "option_b": "SSRIs impair platelet function (serotonin-mediated platelet aggregation) and may reduce PRP effectiveness or increase bleeding risk; this should be communicated to the ordering physician", "option_c": "SSRIs have no effect on platelet function", "option_d": "PRP is contraindicated for all shoulder conditions", "correct_option_index": 0, "rationale": "SSRIs reduce platelet serotonin uptake, impairing serotonin-mediated platelet aggregation. This theoretically reduces PRP efficacy (as PRP relies on platelet growth factor release) and may increase bleeding risk with injection. The physiotherapist should document and communicate this to the physician ordering PRP."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 48-year-old male with a history of gout presents with acute right great toe joint pain, swelling, erythema, and warmth. He is unable to bear weight. His serum uric acid is elevated. The physiotherapist is asked to begin ROM exercises. Which action is MOST appropriate?", "option_a": "Proceed with passive great toe joint mobilization", "option_b": "Defer physiotherapy until the acute gout flare resolves, as joint manipulation during acute crystal arthropathy is contraindicated and will worsen inflammation", "option_c": "Apply ice and immediately begin ROM exercises", "option_d": "Prescribe high-load intrinsic foot exercises", "correct_option_index": 1, "rationale": "Acute gout is an inflammatory crystal arthropathy where any joint stress (including manual therapy or exercise) during an active flare markedly worsens inflammation and pain. Physiotherapy intervention is deferred until the acute episode resolves, typically in 7-14 days with medical management."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist is treating a 35-year-old female with right shoulder impingement. She demonstrates a positive upper cut test (elbow flexed to 90 degrees, forearm supinated, shoulder in 60 degrees of forward flexion — upward force into the physiotherapist's resisting hand reproduces anterior shoulder pain). Which structure is MOST likely irritated?", "option_a": "Supraspinatus tendon", "option_b": "Biceps tendon or superior labrum at the biceps anchor (associated with SLAP pathology)", "option_c": "Infraspinatus tendon", "option_d": "Subscapularis tendon", "correct_option_index": 2, "rationale": "The upper cut test (also called the anterior slide test variant) specifically loads the long head of biceps tendon and biceps anchor at the superior labrum. A positive test (pain in anterior shoulder with upward resisted force in the described position) suggests bicipital tendinopathy or SLAP pathology."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 50-year-old male with advanced COPD and chronic LBP is referred for physiotherapy. His FEV1 is 35% predicted. The physiotherapist plans a rehabilitation program. Which exercise intensity principle is MOST important?", "option_a": "Exercise intensity is not relevant for COPD patients", "option_b": "Exercise should be prescribed and progressed based on Borg dyspnea rating (target 3-4/10) and monitored SpO2, with lower intensity aerobic exercise to avoid desaturation", "option_c": "High-intensity interval training is preferred for all COPD patients", "option_d": "Lumbar exercises can proceed at maximal intensity regardless of respiratory status", "correct_option_index": 3, "rationale": "In severe COPD (FEV1 35% predicted), exercise capacity is significantly limited by ventilation and gas exchange. Exercise intensity must be guided by Borg dyspnea ratings (3-4/10) and SpO2 monitoring (maintain >88-90%). Low-intensity but consistent aerobic exercise is the foundation for functional improvement."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A 26-year-old female presents with intermittent right shoulder pain during swimming. She reports pain at the end of the pull phase. Examination shows excessive scapular internal rotation and posterior tilt deficit during arm elevation. The scapular dyskinesis test is positive. Which describes the MOST likely mechanism of shoulder impingement in swimmers?", "option_a": "Rotator cuff tear from overuse", "option_b": "Scapular upward rotation and posterior tilt deficits that reduce the subacromial space during the swimming pull phase, causing repetitive impingement", "option_c": "Bicipital tendinopathy from repetitive elbow flexion", "option_d": "Glenohumeral instability from hyperlaxity", "correct_option_index": 0, "rationale": "Swimmer's shoulder impingement is primarily driven by scapular dyskinesis (insufficient upward rotation and posterior tilting during arm elevation), which reduces the subacromial space during the pull-through phase. Correcting scapular kinematics through specific lower trapezius and serratus anterior training is the primary treatment target."},
  {"domain": "Musculoskeletal", "template_id": 1, "question_text": "A physiotherapist works in a Canadian rural community. She assesses a 58-year-old male farmer with a new onset of severe LBP after minimal trauma. He has diffuse spinal tenderness, unexpected weight loss of 6 kg over 2 months, fatigue, and a recent diagnosis of multiple myeloma. X-ray shows diffuse osteopenia and a lytic lesion at L2. Which action is MOST appropriate?", "option_a": "Begin lumbar stabilization exercises immediately", "option_b": "Immediate referral to emergency services — this patient has a pathological vertebral fracture risk from myeloma metastasis and requires urgent medical management; physiotherapy is contraindicated until medically cleared", "option_c": "Apply lumbar traction to decompress the L2 lesion", "option_d": "Continue physiotherapy and reassess in 2 weeks", "correct_option_index": 1, "rationale": "Multiple myeloma with a lytic vertebral lesion and unexpected weight loss in an immunocompromised patient constitutes a life-threatening oncological emergency. Pathological fracture risk is extremely high. The physiotherapist must immediately refer to emergency services; any spinal loading or manipulation could cause catastrophic spinal instability."}
